g condition. It's checking `insuredItems.length > 0 && (insuredItems[0].type === \"sword\" || insuredItems[0].type === \"amulet\")`. This is fragile and unclear. A better approach would be to have a function like `hasEquipmentItems(items)` or route based on item types more explicitly.\n\nAlso, `quote` has duplicated `reduce` patterns. Both use `insuredItems.reduce((sum, item) => sum + ..., 0)`. We could extract a helper.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\n\nDuplication observed:\n1. The `reduce` pattern appears twice for baseCents and itemModifierTotal.\n2. `item.type === \"sword\"` and `item.type === \"amulet\"` appear in the routing condition and in `basePremiumFor`.\n\nActually, in `basePremiumFor`, we have:\n```typescript\nif (item.type === \"sword\") return SWORD_PREMIUM;\nif (item.type === \"amulet\") return AMULET_PREMIUM;\nreturn 0;\n```\n\nAnd routing condition uses `item.type === \"sword\" || item.type === \"amulet\"`. We could reuse `basePremiumFor(item) > 0` or have an `isEquipmentItem` helper.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n\nDo we need all the constants? Yes, they reveal intent.\nDo we need both `premiumForComponentCount` and `premiumForComponentsOfType`? They separate concerns, so yes.\n\n### Proposed Refactoring\n\n1. Extract `isEquipmentItem` helper to clarify routing.\n2. Extract `sumBy` helper to reduce duplication in `reduce` calls.\n3. Improve naming/structure of `quote`.\n\nLet me think about this carefully.\n\nActually, I think the biggest improvement is making the routing intent clear. Currently, the first item determines the path. But semantically, the component path is for items that are only runes/moonstones (no equipment), and the equipment path is for equipment items. We could route based on whether ALL items are components.\n\nWait, but the tests don't have mixed component/equipment lists. Let me not change behavior, just improve clarity.\n\nOne approach:\n```typescript\nconst isEquipmentItem = (item: Item): boolean =>\n  item.type === \"sword\" || item.type === \"amulet\";\n\nconst hasEquipmentItems = (items: Item[]): boolean =>\n  items.some(isEquipmentItem);\n\nconst sumBy = <T>(items: T[], fn: (item: T) => number): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + fn(item), 0);\n```\n\nThen `quote` becomes:\n```typescript\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  insuredItems: Item[],\n  contractIndex: number | null = null,\n): number => {\n  if (hasEquipmentItems(insuredItems)) {\n    const baseCents = sumBy(insuredItems, (item) => basePremiumFor(item) * CENTS_PER_GOLD);\n    const itemModifierTotal = sumBy(insuredItems, itemModifierCents);\n    const policyRate = policyModifierRate(customer, contractIndex);\n    const policyModifierCents = baseCents * policyRate;\n    const totalCents =\n      baseCents + itemModifierTotal + policyModifierCents + BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n    return roundUpCentsToGold(totalCents);\n  }\n  return (\n    BASE_FEE +\n    premiumForComponentsOfType(insuredItems, \"rune\") +\n    premiumForComponentsOfType(insuredItems, \"moonstone\")\n  );\n};\n```\n\nBut wait, this changes behavior! Original checks `insuredItems.length > 0 && insuredItems[0].type === ...`. If the first item is a component but later items are equipment, original would take component path. New version would take equipment path.\n\nIs there a test that exercises this? No, not in the current 9 passing tests. But we must keep tests green, and we shouldn't introduce behavior changes that could break future tests.\n\nActually, the spec probably doesn't allow mixed component/equipment lists. But to be safe and not change behavior, I should keep the routing based on first non-empty item. However, I can still make it clearer:\n\n```typescript\nconst firstItem = insuredItems[0];\nconst usesEquipmentPath = firstItem && isEquipmentItem(firstItem);\n```\n\nOr maybe the spec says something about unknown item types and exits. Looking at the todos, there's \"should exit with non-zero status for unknown item