f we use sumBy. I incorrectly said loops reduce to 2. Let me recalculate.\n\nWait, but `premiumForComponentsOfType` calls `countByType` which uses filter. And quote calls `premiumForComponentsOfType` twice. So in the component path:\n- countByType(\"rune\") -> 1 filter\n- countByType(\"moonstone\") -> 1 filter\nTotal: 2 filter loops in component path.\n\nIn equipment path:\n- sumBy(base premium) -> 1 reduce\n- sumBy(item modifier) -> 1 reduce\nTotal: 2 reduce loops.\n\nIn original:\n- quote equipment path: 2 reduce loops\n- quote component path: 0 direct loops, but premiumForComponentsOfType calls countByType twice = 2 filter loops\n\nSo total loops are the same: 2 per path. But the original counted 3 because I counted both reduces in quote (2) + filter in countByType (1) = 3. In the refactored version, sumBy has 1 reduce, quote has 0 direct, countByType has 1 filter. So total = 2 (one reduce + one filter) but there are two sumBy calls when in equipment path... wait.\n\nI think the mass should be calculated on the code structure, not runtime path. So:\n- Original code has `filter` in countByType (1 loop) and 2 `reduce` in quote (2 loops) = 3 loops in code.\n- New code has `filter` in countByType (1 loop) and `reduce` in sumBy (1 loop) = 2 loops in code, even though sumBy is called twice.\n\nSo total loops in code: 2 in new vs 3 in old. That matches my earlier count. But does that make sense? If sumBy is called twice, is it really \"one loop\"? The APP counts code elements, not executions. So yes, 2 loops in new code.\n\nOK so my mass calculation is correct for code structure.\n\nBut the new mass is higher. Let me see if there's a simpler refactoring.\n\nWhat if I just extract the routing condition without sumBy?\n\n```typescript\nconst usesEquipmentPricing = (items: Item[]): boolean =>\n  items.length > 0 && (items[0].type === \"sword\" || items[0].type === \"amulet\");\n```\n\nAnd keep the reduces inline. Then:\n- New bindings: +1 (items param in helper) = 29\n- Ne