ring. However we need ensure we follow simple design rules fully: Rule2 reveals intent; Rule3 no duplication; Rule4 fewest elements. We should evaluate each.\n\nCurrent code after changes: let's read.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:13","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":245,"output":157,"cacheRead":20672,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":137,"totalTokens":21074,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784923968286,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-85522da5-98ac-4044-862b-640890494729","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:13","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"type Item = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\ntype Customer = {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number;\n};\n\nconst CENTS_PER_GOLD = 100;\n\nconst BASE_FEE = 5;\nconst SWORD_PREMIUM = 100;\nconst AMULET_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK = 3;\n\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_YEARS = 2;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst INSURANCE_CONTRACT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\n\nconst countByType = (items: Item[], type: string): number =>\n  items.filter((item) => item.type === type).length;\n\nconst premiumForComponentCount = (count: number): number => {\n  if (count === COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM;\n  }\n  return count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n};\n\nconst premiumForComponentsOfType = (items: Item[], type: string): number =>\n  premiumForComponentCount(countByType(items, type));\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (item: Item): number => {\n  if (item.type === \"sword\") return SWORD_PREMIUM;\n  if (item.type === \"amulet\") return AMULET_PREMIUM;\n  return 0;\n};\n\nconst usesEquipmentPricing = (items: Item[]): boolean =>\n  items.length > 0 && (items[0].type === \"sword\" || items[0].type === \"amulet\");\n\nconst sumBy = <T>(items: T[], valueFor: (item: T) => number): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + valueFor(item), 0);\n\nconst isHighlyEnchanted = (item: Item): boolean =>\n  item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD;\n\nconst itemSurchargeCents = (item: Item): number => {\n  let modifier = 0;\n  const base = basePremiumFor(item);\n  if (item.cursed) {\n    modifier += base * CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  }\n  if (isHighlyEnchanted(item)) {\n    modifier += base * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  }\n  return modifier * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n};\n\nconst roundUpCentsToGold = (cents: number): number =>\n  Math.ceil(cents / CENTS_PER_GOLD);\n\nconst policyAdjustmentRate = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  contractIndex: number | null,\n): number => {\n  const qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_YEARS;\n  const hasInsuranceContract = contractIndex !== null;\n  const isFollowUpContract = contractIndex !== null && contractIndex > 0;\n\n  let rate = 0;\n  if (qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount) {\n    rate -= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  }\n  if (hasInsuranceContract) {\n    rate += INSURANCE_CONTRACT_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  }\n  if (isFollowUpContract) {\n    rate -= FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  }\n  return rate;\n};\n\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  insuredItems: Item[],\n  contractIndex: number | null = null,\n): number => {\n  if (usesEquipmentPricing(insuredItems)) {\n    const baseCents = sumBy(\n      insuredItems,\n      (item) => basePremiumFor(item) * CENTS_PER_GOLD,\n    );\n    const itemModifierTotal = sumBy(insuredItems, itemSurchargeCents);\n    const policyRate = policyAdjustmentRate(customer, contractIndex);\n    const policyModifierCents = baseCents * policyRate;\n    const totalCents =\n      baseCents + itemModifierTotal + policyModifierCents + BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n    return roundUpCentsToGold(totalCents);\n  }\n  return (\n    BASE_FEE +\n    premiumForComponentsOfType(insuredItems, \"rune\") +\n    premiumForComponentsOfType(insuredItems, \"moonstone\")\n  );\n};\n\nexport const claim = (policy: any, incident: any): any => {\n  return undefined;\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784923970301},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now evaluate simple design rules:\n\nRule 1: tests pass - yes.\nRule 2: Reveals intent. Names improved: `itemSurchargeCents`, `policyAdjustmentRate`, `isHighlyEnchanted`, constants named. The `itemModifierTotal` variable still uses old term \"modifier\"; maybe rename to `itemSurchargeTotal` to match. That would improve consistency. Also `policyModifierCents` could be `policyAdjustmentCents`. Let's do that. Also in component branch, type strings `\"rune\"`, `\"moonstone\"` repeated; maybe extract `COMPONENT_TYPES` array. But not necessary. But variable naming inconsistency is a Rule2 issue. We can fix.\nRule 3: No duplication. Magic numbers now named; component type strings duplicated. Could extract constant array. Also `BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD` appears in equipment branch; could extract `BASE_FEE_CENTS` constant. That might reduce duplication and improve intent. Also `contractIndex !== null` appears twice in `policyAdjustmentRate`; we already used `hasInsuranceContract` in one, but `isFollowUpContract` repeats the check. Could reuse `hasInsuranceContract`: `const isFollowUpContract = hasInsuranceContract && contractIndex > 0;`. That removes duplication. Good.\nRule 4: Fewest elements. We added constants and helper. The constants improve clarity; not unnecessary. Could we inline? No.\n\nLet's apply these additional small improvements:\n- Rename `itemModifierTotal` -> `itemSurchargeTotal`.\n- Rename `policyModifierCents` -> `policyAdjustmentCents`.\n- Extract `BASE_FEE_CENTS = BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD`.\n- Reuse `hasInsuranceContract` in `isFollowUpContract`.\n- Extract `COMPONENT_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]` and reduce duplication in quote. But adding array and loop might increase mass; but improves DRY. Since component branch only has two types, duplication is mild. However test list expects unknown type exit maybe later. But not needed now. Could leave.\n\nAlso `usesEquipmentPricing` name: maybe rename to `firstItemIsEquipment`? But the function returns true if first item is sword/amulet, which triggers equipment pricing. The name `usesEquipmentPricing` reveals branch intent. Keep.\n\n`roundUpCentsToGold` is clear.\n\nLet's apply variable rename and `BASE_FEE_CENTS` and reuse hasInsuranceContract. Also maybe extract `componentTypes`? Let's decide. The duplicate strings in quote are a mild duplication. We can extract constants for type names: `RUNE_TYPE = \"rune\"`, `MOONSTONE_TYPE = \"moonstone\"`. But then we have two more constants. Or a `COMPONENT_TYPES` array and use `sumBy` to sum premiums. That would use a loop (mass 5) and call. But overkill. Since Rule4 says fewest elements, maybe leave inline strings. But duplication of literals is a DRY issue. However the strings are domain literals; naming them improves intent. I'd extract `RUNE_TYPE` and `MOONSTONE_TYPE`? But then in `basePremiumFor` we also have `\"sword\"`, `\"amulet\"` literals. Could extract those too. But adding many constants may be too many elements. However for Rule3, naming repeated literals is good. But here they appear only once in code (except `\"rune\"` and `\"moonstone\"` each once). Wait `premiumForComponentsOfType(insuredItems, \"rune\")` appears once; `\"moonstone\"` once. So no duplication. The duplication is conceptual? They are magic strings. But fine.\n\nActually `usesEquipmentPricing` uses `\"sword\"`, `\"amulet\"` once. No duplication.\n\nSo we can