olicy, incident = 3.\n\nSo: 3+2+3+2+2+2+3+2+6+2+5+2+6+8+3 = 51\nPlus const bindings: 17\nTotal bindings: 68\n\nInvocations:\n- items.filter: 1\n- premiumForComponentCount: 1\n- premiumForComponentsOfType: 2\n- isComponentType: 1\n- isKnownItemType: 1\n- items.reduce: 1\n- basePremiumForItem: 2 (one in surchargeCentsFor, one in quote callback)\n- isHighlyEnchanted: 1\n- Math.ceil: 1\n- validateItems: 1\n- sumBy: 2\n- componentBasePremium: 1\n- policyAdjustmentRate: 1\n- roundUpCentsToGold: 1\n- valueFor(item) inside sumBy: 1\nTotal invocations: 1+1+2+1+1+1+2+1+1+1+2+1+1+1+1 = 17\n\nWait, I need to be consistent. The `valueFor(item)` inside sumBy is an invocation. And `surchargeCentsFor` passed to sumBy will be invoked via valueFor. But we count the call expression `valueFor(item)` once.\n\nConditionals:\n- premiumForComponentCount if: 1\n- basePremiumForItem ternary: 1\n- validateItems if: 1\n- surchargeCentsFor cursed ternary: 1\n- surchargeCentsFor enchanted ternary: 1\n- policyAdjustmentRate 3 ternaries: 3\nTotal conditionals: 8\n\nLoops:\n- items.filter: 1\n- items.reduce: 1\n- validateItems for...of: 1\nTotal loops: 3\n\nAssignments: 0 (no mutations)\n\nMass = constants(33) + bindings(68) + invocations(17*2=34) + conditionals(8*4=32) + loops(3*5=15) + assignments(0) = 33+68+34+32+15 = 182.\n\nHmm, that seems really high. I think I'm overcounting. The APP definition is meant to be simple but I'm being very granular. Maybe I should count a `const` declaration as just a binding, not a binding + constant? The constant value is part of the binding. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, in APP terms:\n- A `const x = 5` creates a binding `x` and uses constant `5`. So it contributes 1 binding + 1 constant.\n- A `const obj = { a: 1 }` creates binding `obj`, plus constants `1` and `\"a\"`.\n\nBut maybe the intent is simpler. Let me look at the example given:\n```\nfunction calculate(numbers: number[]): number {\n  return numbers.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0);\n}\nComponent Count:\n- Constants: 1 (literal 0) = 1\n- Bindings: 3 (numbers, sum, num) = 3\n- Invocations: 2 (reduce, +) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (reduce is iteration) = 5\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\nTotal Mass: 13\n```\n\nInteresting: they count `+` as an invocation! They say \"Invocations: 2 (reduce, +)\". So binary operators like `+` are counted as invocations in their interpretation. Also they only count `numbers`, `sum`, `num` as bindings, not `calculate`. Hmm, they didn't count the function name as a binding. Or maybe they don't count function declarations as bindings, just parameters and local variables.\n\nAlso they count `0` as 1 constant, not the `const` binding.\n\nSo following this example's style:\n- Don't count function name bindings (like `calculate`, `sumBy`, etc.)\n- Count parameters and local variables as bindings\n- Count operators like `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `===`, `>=`, `&&`, `||`, `in`? Maybe not `in`. But `+` definitely.\n- Count `reduce` and `filter` and `for` as loops.\n\nLet me recalculate following the example's apparent conventions.\n\nBut wait, the example is a simple one. I need to be consistent. Let me use this interpretation:\n- Constants: literal values\n- Bindings: parameters and local variables declared with const/let (not function names)\n- Invocations: function calls AND binary operators? Actually, in the example they say `reduce` and `+` are 2 invocations. So yes, arithmetic/logical/comparison operators are invocations in their model.\n- Conditionals: if/switch/ternary\n- Loops: for/forEach/map/reduce/filter\n- Assignments: mutations (not const declarations)\n\nThis will be a huge mass. Let me try to be reasonable. Actually, counting every `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `===`, `>=`, `&&`, `||`, `in`, `?:` etc. as invocations would make the mass enormous.\n\nHmm, the example says \"Invocations: 2 (reduce, +)\". Maybe they only count `+` as invocation because addition is a function-like operation. But `===`? Maybe not.\n\nI think the APP model is intentionally fuzzy, and the key is consistency. Since I'm the one doing the calculation, I should document my counting method clearly.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll use a pragmatic approach:\n1. Count literal values as constants\n2. Count function parameters and local variables as bindings\n3. Count function calls as invocations\n4. Count control flow (if, ternary) as conditionals\n5. Count explicit iteration (for, forEach, reduce, filter, map) as loops\n6. Count mutations as assignments\n7. Ignore operators for simplicity, except function calls\n\nBut then the example's \"Invocations: 2 (reduce, +)\" would differ from mine. I'll document that I count function calls only.\n\nActually, let me look at the APP more carefully. It says \"Invocation (Mass: 2): Function calls (`calculate()`, `Math.max()`).\" The example says `+` is an invocation. This is contradictory. I'll follow the definition text, not the example, because the text says \"Function calls\".\n\nOK so my count:\n- Constants: literal values (numbers, strings, null, undefined, booleans)\n- Bindings: parameters + local const/let variables\n- Invocations: function calls only\n- Conditionals: if/switch/ternary\n- Loops: for/forEach/reduce/filter/map\n- Assignments: mutations\n\nLet me recount with this method, excluding function names as bindings (following the example).\n\nConstants:\nNumeric literals: 100, 5, 100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 60, 3, 2, 0.2, 0.1, 0.15, 5, 0.5, 0.3 = 16\nObject keys as string literals: \"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\", \"potion\" = 4\n\"rune\" x 2, \"moonstone\" x 2 = 4\n0 literals: in basePremiumForItem (1), surchargeCentsFor (2), policyAdjustmentRate (3) = 6\nnull literal: 1\nundefined literals: 2\nTotal constants: 16+4+4+6+1+2 = 33\n\nBindings (parameters + locals, excluding function names):\ncountByType: items, type, item = 3\npremiumForComponentCount: count = 1\npremiumForComponentsOfType: items, type = 2\nbasePremiumForItem: item = 1\nisComponentType: type = 1\nisKnownItemType: type = 1\nvalidateItems: items, item = 2\ncomponentBasePremium: items = 1\nsumBy: items, valueFor, sum, item = 4 (exclude T and sumBy)\nisHighlyEnchanted: item = 1\nsurchargeCentsFor: item, base, cursedSurcharge, enchantedSurcharge = 4\nroundUpCentsToGold: cents = 1\npolicyAdjustmentRate: customer, contractIndex, qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount, hasInsuranceContract, isFollowUpContract = 5\nquote: customer, insuredItems, contractIndex, baseCents, itemSurchargeTotal, policyRate, policyAdjustmentCents, totalCents = 8\nclaim: policy, incident = 2\nTotal bindings: 3+1+2+1+1+1+2+1+4+1+4+1+5+8+2 = 37\n\nInvocations (function calls):\nitems.filter = 1\npremiumForComponentCount = 1\npremiumForComponentsOfType x 2 = 2\nisComponentType = 1\nisKnownItemType = 1\nitems.reduce = 1\nbasePremiumForItem x 2 = 2\nisHighlyEnchanted = 1\nMath.ceil = 1\nvalidateItems = 1\nsumBy x 2 = 2\ncomponentBasePremium = 1\npolicyAdjustmentRate = 1\nroundUpCentsToGold = 1\nvalueFor(item) in sumBy = 1\nTotal invocations: 1+1+2+1+1+1+2+1+1+1+2+1+1+1+1 = 17\n\nConditionals:\nif in premiumForComponentCount = 1\nternary in basePremiumForItem = 1\nif in validateItems = 1\nternary cursed in surchargeCentsFor = 1\nternary enchanted in surchargeCentsFor = 1\nternaries in policyAdjustmentRate = 3\nTotal conditionals: 8\n\nLoops:\nitems.filter = 1\nitems.reduce = 1\nfor...of in validateItems = 1\nTotal loops: 3\n\nAssignments: 0\n\nTotal mass = 33 + 37 + (17*2) + (8*4) + (3*5) + 0 = 33+37+34+32+15 = 151\n\nOK, mass = 151. That's our baseline.\n\nNow what refactoring can we do?\n\n## Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n\nNames seem mostly clear. One thing: `policyAdjustmentRate` returns a net rate that can be negative (discount) or positive (surcharge). The name is OK but could be more descriptive. However, this is a minor issue.\n\n## Rule 3: No Duplication\n\nDuplications:\n1. `\"rune\"` and `\"moonstone\"` are hardcoded in multiple places:\n   - `isComponentType`\n   - `componentBasePremium`\n   \n   We could extract a `COMPONENT_TYPES` array.\n\n2. The pattern `condition ? value : 0` appears 3 times in `policyAdjustmentRate` and 2 times in `surchargeCentsFor`. We could extract a helper `when(condition, value)` but that might hurt clarity.\n\n3. The pattern of checking `item.enchantment !== undefined` - could be part of `isHighlyEnchanted` only.\n\n## Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n\nCould we reduce the number of functions? Maybe, but the decomposition is reasonable.\n\n## Potential Refactorings\n\n### Refactoring 1: Extract component types constant\n\nReplace hardcoded `\"rune\"` and `\"moonstone\"` with a `COMPONENT_TYPES` array.\n\nBefore:\n```\nconst isComponentType = (type: string): boolean =>\n  type === \"rune\" || type === \"moonstone\";\n\nconst componentBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  premiumForComponentsOfType(items, \"rune\") +\n  premiumForComponentsOfType(items, \"moonstone\");\n```\n\nAfter:\n```\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\n\nconst isComponentType = (type: string): boolean =>\n  COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(type);\n\nconst componentBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  COMPONENT_TYPES.reduce(\n    (sum, type) => sum + premiumForComponentsOfType(items, type),\n    0,\n  );\n```\n\nThis removes duplication of component type names. But it introduces a loop and a reduce. Let me check if mass goes up or down.\n\nActually, `isComponentType` changes from:\n- constants: 2 (\"rune\", \"moonstone\")\n- bindings: 1 (type)\n- invocations: 0\n- conditionals: 0\n- loops: 0\nTo:\n- constants: 0 (the strings move to COMPONENT_TYPES constant)\n- bindings: 1 (type) + COMPONENT_TYPES binding\n- invocations: 1 (includes)\n- conditionals: 0\n- loops: 0\nPlus COMPONENT_TYPES constant adds 1 binding and 2 constants and maybe a loop initializer? No, array literal is just constants.\n\nActually, `COMPONENT_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]` adds:\n- 1 binding (COMPONENT_TYPES)\n- 2 constants (\"rune\", \"moonstone\")\n\nAnd `componentBasePremium` changes from:\n- constants: 2 (\"rune\", \"moonstone\")\n- bindings: 1 (items)\n- invocations: 2 (premiumForComponentsOfType x2)\n- conditionals: 0\n- loops: 0\nTo:\n- constants: 0\n- bindings: 3 (items, sum, type)\n- invocations: 3 (reduce, premiumForComponentsOfType)\n- conditionals: 0\n- loops: 1 (reduce)\n\nSo delta:\n+ COMPONENT_TYPES binding: +1\n+ COMPONENT_TYPES constants: +2\n- isComponentType constants: -2\n- componentBasePremium constants: -2\n+ componentBasePremium bindings: +2 (sum, type)\n+ componentBasePremium invocations: +1 (reduce)\n+ componentBasePremium loops: +1 (+5)\nNet: 1 + 2 - 2 - 2 + 2 + 1 + 5 = +7\n\nMass increases. But clarity improves because component types are centralized. Rule 2 trumps mass. But is the improvement worth the mass increase? It's a small clarity improvement.\n\nHmm, but maybe I can keep `componentBasePremium` with explicit calls and just extract the array for `isComponentType`? That would reduce duplication only slightly. Actually that doesn't help much.\n\n### Refactoring 2: Extract `when` helper for conditional value\n\n```\nconst when = (condition: boolean, value: number): number =>\n  condition ? value : 0;\n```\n\nThis would replace 5 ternaries. But it adds a function and might hurt clarity. Let me not do this.\n\n### Refactoring 3: Inline `isKnownItemType` and `isComponentType`\n\nThese are small. But inlining might reduce mass but hurt clarity. Keep them.\n\n### Refactoring 4: Use a `Set` for known item types\n\nCreate a combined structure for all known types. This might centralize knowledge.\n\n### Refactoring 5: Improve `componentBasePremium` to iterate over component types\n\nSame as Refactoring 1.\n\n### Refactoring 6: Rename `policyAdjustmentRate` to `netPolicyAdjustmentRate`\n\nClarity improvement, no mass change.\n\n### Refactoring 7: Extract a function for total base premium in `quote`\n\n```\nconst basePremiumCentsFor = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  sumBy(items, (item) => basePremiumForItem(item) * CENTS_PER_GOLD) +\n  componentBasePremium(items) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n```\n\nThis adds a function but makes `quote` clearer. Mass increase.\n\n### Refactoring 8: Combine equipment base premiums and component types\n\n```\nconst ITEM_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: SWORD_PREMIUM,\n  amulet: AMULET_PREMIUM,\n  staff: STAFF_PREMIUM,\n  potion: POTION_PREMIUM,\n  rune: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n  moonstone: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n};\n```\n\nThen component block logic needs special handling. This might be a bigger change.\n\n### Refactoring 9: Simplify `surchargeCentsFor` using arrays\n\n```\nconst surchargeCentsFor = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumForItem(item);\n  const surcharges = [\n    item.cursed ? CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0,\n    isHighlyEnchanted(item) ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0,\n  ];\n  const totalRate = surcharges.reduce((sum, rate) => sum + rate, 0);\n  return base * totalRate * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n};\n```\n\nThis changes structure but may not improve clarity. It introduces a loop.\n\n### Refactoring 10: Make `basePremiumForItem` handle components too\n\nCurrently components have base premium 0 and are handled separately. We could unify:\n```\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: Item): number =>\n  EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n```\n\nBut then component block discount wouldn't work. We'd need to handle components differently.\n\nActually, a better idea: use a single premium lookup table including components, and handle block discount separately.\n\n```\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: SWORD_PREMIUM,\n  amulet: AMULET_PREMIUM,\n  staff: STAFF_PREMIUM,\n  potion: POTION_PREMIUM,\n  rune: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n  moonstone: COMPONENT_PREMIUM,\n};\n```\n\nThen:\n- `basePremiumForItem` returns `BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0` (or throw)\n- `isKnownItemType` checks `item.type in BASE_PREMIUMS`\n- Component block discount: for components, if count is 3, premium is 60 not 75. So we need special logic.\n\nThis could reduce duplication because `EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS`, `isComponentType`, and `componentBasePremium` all relate to the same type classification. But it's a bigger change and might break tests.\n\nLet me think about what small, safe refactoring I can do that improves the code.\n\n### Refactoring 11: Extract `COMPONENT_TYPES` constant and use it in both places\n\nThis is the cleanest small refactoring. It centralizes the list of component types. Even though mass increases slightly, it improves maintainability and reveals intent (these are the component types).\n\nLet me implement this and calculate mass.\n\nActually, I want to think if there's a way to reduce mass while improving clarity.\n\n### Refactoring 12: Remove redundant constants\n\nSome constants are used only once or could be inlined. For example:\n- `CENTS_PER_GOLD` is used in multiple places, keep it.\n- `COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK` is used once in `premiumForComponentCount`, but it's a meaningful constant, keep it.\n\n### Refactoring 13: Use `every` instead of `for...of` in validateItems\n\n```\nconst validateItems = (items: Item[]): void => {\n  const unknownType = items.find((item) => !isKnownItemType(item.type));\n  if (unknownType) {\n    throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${unknownType.type}`);\n  }\n};\n```\n\nThis replaces a loop + conditional with an invocation + conditional. But `find` is also a loop. Mass might stay similar. Clarity improves slightly.\n\nBefore validateItems:\n- bindings: items, item = 2\n- loop: 1\n- conditional: 1\n- invocation: 1\nMass contribution: 2 + 5 + 4 + 2 = 13\n\nAfter:\n- bindings: items, unknownType = 2\n- loop: 1 (find)\n- conditional: 1\n- invocation: 2 (find, isKnownItemType)\nMass contribution: 2 + 5 + 4 + 4 = 15\n\nMass increases by 2. But intent might be clearer? Not really.\n\n### Refactoring 14: Combine `isKnownItemType` and `validateItems` logic\n\nNot necessary.\n\n### Refactoring 15: Improve `basePremiumForItem` using nullish coalescing\n\n```\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: Item): number =>\n  EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0;\n```\n\nThis is clearer than `in` + ternary. But does it change mass?\n\nBefore:\n- bindings: item = 1\n- conditional: 1 (ternary)\n- constants: 0 = 1\nMass: 1 + 4 + 1 = 6\n\nAfter:\n- bindings: item = 1\n- conditional: 0\n- constants: 0 = 1\nMass: 1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n\nMass decreases by 4! And clarity improves (nullish coalescing is modern and clear). But `??` is an operator, not an invocation/conditional in my counting. \n\nWait, but `EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type]` when the key doesn't exist returns `undefined`, and `?? 0` gives 0. This is equivalent to the current logic. Good.\n\nBut I need to be careful: if the value were `undefined` or `null`, `??` would also return 0. But our values are numbers, so it's fine.\n\nThis is a good refactoring! It reduces mass and improves clarity. However, the `in` operator + ternary might be considered more explicit. But `??` is standard TypeScript.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The APP guidelines say \"Rule 2 trumps APP: Clarity over low mass\". But `EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0` is arguably clearer than the ternary. It's concise and idiomatic.\n\nLet me also consider: this change affects only `basePremiumForItem`. It's a small refactoring but valid.\n\n### Refactoring 16: Simplify `premiumForComponentCount`\n\nCurrently:\n```\nconst premiumForComponentCount = (count: number): number => {\n  if (count === COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM;\n  }\n  return count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n};\n```\n\nCould use ternary:\n```\nconst premiumForComponentCount = (count: number): number =>\n  count === COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n```\n\nThis removes a function-body-with-block and replaces if with ternary. Mass stays same (1 conditional either way). Slightly more concise. But not a huge improvement.\n\n### Refactoring 17: Simplify `policyAdjustmentRate`\n\nWe could extract 