mponentType + COMPONENT_TYPES for both: mass 156, fully centralizes component type knowledge\n- Option D: inline isComponentType only: mass 144, removes abstraction but keeps duplication\n\nOption B seems balanced: reduces mass and improves maintainability by centralizing component type list in `isKnownItemType`. But `componentBasePremium` still duplicates. That's not ideal.\n\nOption C fully centralizes but increases mass. Rule 2 trumps mass, but the clarity gain from full centralization might not justify the mass increase.\n\nActually, the most important thing is to make the code reveal intent and reduce duplication. The duplication of component type strings is a real issue. Let me go with Option C and accept the mass increase, documenting that the trade-off is justified by reduced duplication and improved maintainability.\n\nWait, but the instructions say \"Lower mass = Better code (generally)\" and \"Rule 2 trumps APP: Clarity over low mass\". If the refactoring improves clarity/maintainability, mass increase is acceptable.\n\nLet me think about whether the reduce in `componentBasePremium` actually improves clarity. Old code:\n```\npremiumForComponentsOfType(items, \"rune\") +\npremiumForComponentsOfType(items, \"moonstone\")\n```\nNew code:\n```\nCOMPONENT_TYPES.reduce(\n  (sum, type) => sum + premiumForComponentsOfType(items, type),\n  0,\n)\n```\n\nThe old code is very explicit: \"premium for runes plus premium for moonstones\". The new code is more abstract: \"sum premiums for each component type\". For someone reading the code, the old code might actually be clearer because it explicitly shows the two types. The new code requires looking up COMPONENT_TYPES.\n\nSo maybe the reduce doesn't improve clarity. It reduces duplication but at a clarity cost. Rule 2 > Rule 3, so we should prefer clarity over DRY in this case.\n\nTherefore, Option B is better: use COMPONENT_TYPES in `isKnownItemType` (where it clearly means \"any component type\"), but keep `component