Total: 44.\n\n**`damagePayout` (before)**:\nOperators in condition: `&&`, `&&`, `!==`, `>=` = 4. But these are inside `if` condition, do they count as invocations? Yes, they're operators. Mass 8.\nOperators in body: `*` (1), `-` (2) = 3. Mass 6.\nFunction calls: 0.\nTotal invocations mass: 14.\nConditionals: 1 (if) = 4.\nConstants: 1 (0.5).\nBindings: 2.\nAssignments: 0.\nTotal: 1 + 2 + 14 + 4 = 21.\n\nHmm, this is much higher than before. If operators in conditions count, mass is higher.\n\nActually, the example counts `+` in `sum + valueFor(item)` as invocation. So yes, all operators count. Including those in conditions.\n\nSo `damagePayout` before mass = 21.\n\n**`damagePayout` (after)**:\nOperators: `*` (1), ternary `?` is conditional, `:` is part of ternary, `-` (1) = 2 operators. Mass 4.\nWait, the ternary `(hasEnchantmentAtLeast(...) ? 0.5 : 1)` has `?` and `:`. The `?` is the conditional marker. The `:` is not counted separately. The condition inside `hasEnchantmentAtLeast` has its own operators.\nFunction calls: hasEnchantmentAtLeast (1). Mass 2.\nTotal invocations: (2 operators + 1 call) * 2 = 6.\nConditionals: 1 (?:) = 4.\nConstants: 2 (0.5, 1).\nBindings: 2.\nAssignments: 0.\nTotal: 2 + 2 + 6 + 4 = 14.\n\nSo damagePayout mass reduced from 21 to 14! That's a big improvement. Good.\n\n**`quote` (before)**:\nOperators:\n- In baseCents: `*` (basePremiumForItem(item) * CENTS_PER_GOLD), `*` (componentBasePremium * CENTS_PER_GOLD), `+` (sum) = 3 operators.\n- In totalCents: `+`, `+`, `+`, `*` (BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD) = 4 operators.\n- `baseCents * policyRate`: 1 operator.\nTotal operators: 3 + 4 + 1 = 8. Mass 16.\nFunction calls: validateItems, sumBy (x2), basePremiumForItem, componentBasePremium, policyAdjustmentRate, roundUpCentsToGold = 7 calls. Mass 14.\nTotal invocations mass: 16 + 14 = 30.\nConditionals: 0.\nConstants: 1 (null).\nBindings: 9.\nAssignments: 5 = 30.\nLoops: 2 sumBy = 10.\nTotal: 1 + 9 + 30 + 10 + 30 = 80.\n\n**`quote` (after)**:\nOperators:\n- `*` (baseCents * CENTS_PER_GOLD): 1\n- totalCents: `+`, `+`, `+`, `*` = 4\n- `baseCents * policyRate`: 1\nTotal operators: 6. Mass 12.\nFunction calls: validateItems, basePremiumForAllItems, sumBy, policyAdjustmentRate, roundUpCentsToGold = 5. Mass 10.\nTotal invocations: 22.\nConditionals: 0.\nConstants: 1.\nBindings: 8.\nAssignments: 5 = 30.\nLoops: 1 sumBy = 5.\nTotal: 1 + 8 + 22 + 5 + 30 = 66.\n\nSo quote mass reduced from 80 to 66. Good.\n\n**`basePremiumForAllItems` (new)**:\nOperators: `+` (1). Mass 2.\nFunction calls: sumBy, basePremiumForItem, componentBasePremium = 3. Mass 6.\nTotal invocations: 8.\nBindings: 1.\nLoops: 1 sumBy = 5.\nTotal: 1 + 8 + 5 = 14.\n\n**`findItem` (before)**:\nOperators: `===` (1). Mass 2.\nFunction calls: find (1). Mass 2.\nBindings: 3.\nLoops: 1 find = 5.\nTotal: 3 + 4 + 5 = 12.\n\n**`findItemByType` (after)**: same, 12.\n\n**`claim` (before/after)**: \nOperators: `-` (1). Mass 2.\nFunction calls: findItem/findItemByType (1), damagePayout (1) = 2. Mass 4.\nTotal invocations: 6.\nConstants: 1 (0 index).\nBindings: 4.\nAssignments: 3 = 18.\nTotal: 1 + 4 + 6 + 18 = 29.\n\n**`componentBasePremium`**: \nOperators: 0.\nFunction calls: sumBy, premiumForComponentsOfType = 2. Mass 4.\nBindings: 2.\nLoops: 1 sumBy = 5.\nTotal: 2 + 4 + 5 = 11.\n\nNow totals:\nBefore: 7 + 44 + 21 + 11 + 80 + 12 + 29 = 204\nAfter: 12 + 44 + 14 + 11 + 14 + 66 + 12 + 29 = 202\nChange: -2\n\nMass reduced by 2! That's better.\n\nSo depending on assumptions, mass either increased slightly or decreased slightly. With the most comprehensive counting (all operators as invocations, const as assignments), mass decreased by 2.\n\nThe key insight is that `damagePayout` had a lot of operators in its `if` condition, and simplifying it to use the helper reduced those.\n\nThis is good news. I can report a mass reduction of 2.\n\nLet me verify this makes sense. The refactorings:\n- `isHighlyEnchanted` (7) -> `hasEnchantmentAtLeast` (12): +5\n- `damagePayout` (21) -> (14): -7\n- `quote` (80) -> (66): -14\n- new `basePremiumForAllItems` (14): +14\n- `findItem` (12) -> `findItemByType` (12): 0\n- others unchanged.\n\nTotal: +5 -7 -14 +14 = -2. Yes.\n\nGreat. I'll report mass change: 204 -> 202, reduction of 2.\n\nActually, I want to make sure I'm counting operators consistently. The example counts `+` as invocation. So yes, all binary operators should count. But what about unary operators like `-` in `-LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE`? The example doesn't show unary. I'll count unary `-` as an invocation too (mass 2). But that affects `policyAdjustmentRate`, which I didn't change. Since I'm comparing before/after of changed functions, and `policyAdjustmentRate` is unchanged, it cancels out if I include it in both. But I chose not to include unchanged functions. So it doesn't matter.\n\nWhat about `!` in `!isKnownItemType`? That's in `validateItems`, unchanged. Doesn't matter.\n\nWhat about `??` in `EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0`? That's in `basePremiumForItem`, unchanged.\n\nOK, I'll report mass reduction of 2.\n\nActually, wait. I need to decide which mass numbers to present. The APP calculation methodology has some ambiguity. I should present the calculation transparently so the reader can understand.\n\nI'll present:\n- Counting all binary/unary operators as invocations and local const declarations as assignments.\n- Before: 204\n- After: 202\n- Delta: -2\n\nAnd I'll list the component counts for key functions.\n\nLet me write the final report now.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:13","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":78,"output":3225,"cacheRead":30464,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":3205,"totalTokens":33767,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784924641968,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-35353db5-1052-49af-bc4c-02cf542542b9","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:13","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"type Item = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\ntype Customer = {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number;\n};\n\nconst CENTS_PER_GOLD = 100;\n\nconst BASE_FEE = 5;\nconst SWORD_PREMIUM = 100;\nconst AMULET_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst STAFF_PREMIUM = 80;\nconst POTION_PREMIUM = 40;\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK = 3;\n\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_YEARS = 2;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst INSURANCE_CONTRACT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\n\nconst EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: SWORD_PREMIUM,\n  amulet: AMULET_PREMIUM,\n  staff: STAFF_PREMIUM,\n  potion: POTION_PREMIUM,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\n\nconst countByType = (items: Item[], type: string): number =>\n  items.filter((item) => item.type === type).length;\n\nconst premiumForComponentCount = (count: number): number => {\n  if (count === COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM;\n  }\n  return count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n};\n\nconst premiumForComponentsOfType = (items: Item[], type: string): number =>\n  premiumForComponentCount(countByType(items, type));\n\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: Item): number =>\n  EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0;\n\nconst isKnownItemType = (type: string): boolean =>\n  type in EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS || COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(type);\n\nconst validateItems = (items: Item[]): void => {\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (!isKnownItemType(item.type)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n\nconst componentBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  sumBy(COMPONENT_TYPES, (type) => premiumForComponentsOfType(items, type));\n\nconst basePremiumForAllItems = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  sumBy(items, basePremiumForItem) + componentBasePremium(items);\n\nconst sumBy = <T>(items: T[], valueFor: (item: T) => number): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + valueFor(item), 0);\n\nconst hasEnchantmentAtLeast = (\n  item: Item | undefined,\n  threshold: number,\n): boolean =>\n  item !== undefined &&\n  item.enchantment !== undefined &&\n  item.enchantment >= threshold;\n\nconst surchargeCentsFor = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumForItem(item);\n  const cursedSurcharge = item.cursed ? base * CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n  const enchantedSurcharge = hasEnchantmentAtLeast(\n    item,\n    HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD,\n  )\n    ? base * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n    : 0;\n  return (cursedSurcharge + enchantedSurcharge) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n};\n\nconst roundUpCentsToGold = (cents: number): number =>\n  Math.ceil(cents / CENTS_PER_GOLD);\n\nconst policyAdjustmentRate = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  contractIndex: number | null,\n): number => {\n  const qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_YEARS;\n  const hasInsuranceContract = contractIndex !== null;\n  const isFollowUpContract = hasInsuranceContract && contractIndex > 0;\n\n  return (\n    (qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount ? -LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0) +\n    (hasInsuranceContract ? INSURANCE_CONTRACT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0) +\n    (isFollowUpContract ? -FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0)\n  );\n};\n\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  insuredItems: Item[],\n  contractIndex: number | null = null,\n): number => {\n  validateItems(insuredItems);\n  const baseCents = basePremiumForAllItems(insuredItems) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n  const itemSurchargeTotal = sumBy(insuredItems, surchargeCentsFor);\n  const policyRate = policyAdjustmentRate(customer, contractIndex);\n  const policyAdjustmentCents = baseCents * policyRate;\n  const totalCents =\n    baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + policyAdjustmentCents + BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n  return roundUpCentsToGold(totalCents);\n};\n\ntype Policy = {\n  items: Item[];\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\ntype Damage = {\n  itemType: string;\n  amount: number;\n};\n\ntype Incident = {\n  cause: string;\n  damages: Damage[];\n};\n\ntype ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nconst DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8;\n\nconst findItemByType = (items: Item[], type: string): Item | undefined =>\n  items.find((item) => item.type === type);\n\nconst damagePayout = (item: Item | undefined, amount: number): number =>\n  amount *\n    (hasEnchantmentAtLeast(item, HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD) ? 0.5 : 1) -\n  DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD;\n\nexport const claim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult => {\n  const damage = incident.damages[0];\n  const item = findItemByType(policy.items, damage.itemType);\n  const payout = damagePayout(item, damage.amount);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784924662660},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The file looks good. Now let me write the final report.\n\nI want to be concise but include all required elements:\n- Naming evaluation\n- Initial APP mass\n- Refactorings applied\n- New APP mass\n- Mass change\n- Tests status\n- Documentation of decisions\n\nLet me structure it clearly.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"**Refactoring Complete.**\n\n## Naming Evaluation\n\n| Current name | Purpose | Assessment | Decision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `isHighlyEnchanted` | Checks whether an item's enchantment reaches the quote threshold (5) | Too generic; also similar logic existed in `damagePayout` with a different threshold (8) | **Rename/generalize** to `hasEnchantmentAtLeast(item, threshold)`