"responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.subagent:143","toolName":"subagent","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Refactoring Complete.\n\n## Naming Evaluation\n\n- `quote` / `claim` — concise domain names, kept.\n- `surchargeCentsFor` — clearly returns cents, kept.\n- `damagePayout` — calculates payout for a damaged item, kept.\n- **Decision:** Names are adequate. Added helper functions (`toCents`, `payoutForDamage`) to reveal intent where raw arithmetic / nested lookups obscured the purpose.\n\n## Initial APP Mass (changed functions)\n\n| Function | Constants | Bindings | Invocations | Conditionals | Loops | Assignments | Subtotal |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `surchargeCentsFor` | 0 | 2 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 10 |\n| `quote` | 1 | 10 | 11 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 33 |\n| `damagePayout` | 0 | 3 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n| `claim` | 0 | 6 | 7 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 20 |\n| **Total** | **1** | **21** | **25** | **0** | **0** | **0** | **72** |\n\n## Refactorings Applied\n\n1. **Extracted `toCents(gold)` helper** — centralizes gold-to-cents conversion.\n2. **Introduced `baseFeeCents`** in `quote` — makes the base fee conversion explicit.\n3. **Extracted `payoutForDamage(items, damage)`** — replaces nested `damagePayout(findItemByType(...), ...)` inside `claim`.\n\n## New APP Mass (changed functions)\n\n| Function | Constants | Bindings | Invocations | Conditionals | Loops | Assignments | Subtotal |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `toCents` | 0 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 4 |\n| `surchargeCentsFor` | 0 | 1 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n| `quote` | 1 | 9 | 11 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 32 |\n| `damagePayout` | 0 | 3 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 9 |\n| `payoutForDamage` | 0 | 2 | 2 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 6 |\n| `claim` | 0 | 6 | 6 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 18 |\n| **Total** | **1** | **23** | **27** | **0** | **0** | **0** | **78** |\n\n**Mass Change:** 72 → 78 (+6)\n\n## Rule Evaluation\n\n- **Rule 1 — Tests Pass:** ✅ All 28 tests pass.\n- **Rule 2 — Reveals Intent:** ✅ `toCents(...)` and `payoutForDamage(...)` make the conversion and lookup/payout steps explicit.\n- **Rule 3 — No Duplication:** ✅ The gold-to-cents multiplication is no longer repeated inline.\n- **Rule 4 — Fewest Elements:** ⚠️ Added two small helpers; accepted because Rule 2 and Rule 3 take priority over minimal mass.\n\n## Decision\n\nAccepted a modest APP mass increase (+6) because the extracted helpers improve intent revelation and remove scattered conversion logic. No test failures were introduced.\n\n**Tests:** 28 passed, 2 todo."}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-41-05_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/claim-office.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim-office.ts\nPassing tests: 28\nRecent Green phase: Applied Math.floor to capped payout\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: src/claim-office.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim-office.ts\nPassing tests: 28\nRecent Green phase: Applied Math.floor to capped payout\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."}],"timestamp":1784925006567},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to refactor the implementation in `src/claim-office.ts` while keeping all tests green. I need to:\n\n1. Read the test file and implementation file\n2. Evaluate naming first\n3. Calculate APP mass before\n4. Apply Simple Design Rules in priority order\n5. Make at least one refactoring attempt\n6. Run tests to ensure they stay green\n7. Calculate APP mass after\n8. Document decisions\n\nLet me start by reading the files.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:0","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:1","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.spec.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":78,"output":137,"cacheRead":5568,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":99,"totalTokens":5783,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784925006587,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-15155fce-bacb-46c7-90c0-252cd7739024","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:0","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"type Item = {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n};\n\ntype Customer = {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number;\n};\n\nconst CENTS_PER_GOLD = 100;\n\nconst BASE_FEE = 5;\nconst SWORD_PREMIUM = 100;\nconst AMULET_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst STAFF_PREMIUM = 80;\nconst POTION_PREMIUM = 40;\nconst COMPONENT_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK = 3;\n\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_YEARS = 2;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst INSURANCE_CONTRACT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\n\nconst EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: SWORD_PREMIUM,\n  amulet: AMULET_PREMIUM,\n  staff: STAFF_PREMIUM,\n  potion: POTION_PREMIUM,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\n\nconst countWhere = <T>(items: T[], predicate: (item: T) => boolean): number =>\n  items.filter(predicate).length;\n\nconst premiumForComponentCount = (count: number): number => {\n  if (count === COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM;\n  }\n  return count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n};\n\nconst premiumForComponentsOfType = (items: Item[], type: string): number =>\n  premiumForComponentCount(\n    countWhere(items, (item) => item.type === type),\n  );\n\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: Item): number =>\n  EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0;\n\nconst isKnownItemType = (type: string): boolean =>\n  type in EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS || COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(type);\n\nconst validateKnownItemTypes = (items: Item[]): void => {\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (!isKnownItemType(item.type)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n\nconst componentPremiumTotal = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  sumBy(COMPONENT_TYPES, (type) => premiumForComponentsOfType(items, type));\n\nconst basePremiumForAllItems = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  sumBy(items, basePremiumForItem) + componentPremiumTotal(items);\n\nconst sumBy = <T>(items: T[], valueFor: (item: T) => number): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + valueFor(item), 0);\n\nconst hasEnchantmentAtLeast = (\n  item: Item | undefined,\n  threshold: number,\n): boolean =>\n  item !== undefined &&\n  item.enchantment !== undefined &&\n  item.enchantment >= threshold;\n\nconst surchargeRateFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  (item.cursed ? CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0) +\n  (hasEnchantmentAtLeast(item, HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD)\n    ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n    : 0);\n\nconst surchargeCentsFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  basePremiumForItem(item) * surchargeRateFor(item) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n\nconst roundUpCentsToGold = (cents: number): number =>\n  Math.ceil(cents / CENTS_PER_GOLD);\n\nconst policyAdjustmentRate = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  contractIndex: number | null,\n): number => {\n  const qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_YEARS;\n  const hasInsuranceContract = contractIndex !== null;\n  const isFollowUpContract = hasInsuranceContract && contractIndex > 0;\n\n  return (\n    (qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount ? -LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0) +\n    (hasInsuranceContract ? INSURANCE_CONTRACT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0) +\n    (isFollowUpContract ? -FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0)\n  );\n};\n\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  insuredItems: Item[],\n  contractIndex: number | null = null,\n): number => {\n  validateKnownItemTypes(insuredItems);\n  const baseCents = basePremiumForAllItems(insuredItems) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n  const itemSurchargeTotal = sumBy(insuredItems, surchargeCentsFor);\n  const policyRate = policyAdjustmentRate(customer, contractIndex);\n  const policyAdjustmentCents = baseCents * policyRate;\n  const totalCents =\n    baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + policyAdjustmentCents + BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n  return roundUpCentsToGold(totalCents);\n};\n\ntype Policy = {\n  items: Item[];\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\ntype Damage = {\n  itemType: string;\n  amount: number;\n};\n\ntype Incident = {\n  cause: string;\n  damages: Damage[];\n};\n\ntype ClaimResult = {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nconst DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8;\n\nconst findItemByType = (items: Item[], type: string): Item | undefined =>\n  items.find((item) => item.type === type);\n\nconst payoutMultiplierFor = (item: Item | undefined): number =>\n  hasEnchantmentAtLeast(item, HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD) ? 0.5 : 1;\n\nconst damagePayout = (item: Item | undefined, amount: number): number =>\n  amount * payoutMultiplierFor(item) - DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD;\n\nconst validateClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): void => {\n  const damageTypes = new Set(incident.damages.map((damage) => damage.itemType));\n  for (const type of damageTypes) {\n    const insuredCount = countWhere(\n      policy.items,\n      (item) => item.type === type,\n    );\n    const damageCount = countWhere(\n      incident.damages,\n      (damage) => damage.itemType === type,\n    );\n    if (damageCount > insuredCount) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage count exceeds insured count for ${type}`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n\nexport const claim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult => {\n  validateClaim(policy, incident);\n  const totalPayout = sumBy(incident.damages, (damage) =>\n    damagePayout(findItemByType(policy.items, damage.itemType), damage.amount),\n  );\n  const cappedPayout = Math.min(totalPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  const roundedPayout = Math.floor(cappedPayout);\n  return { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - roundedPayout };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784925008124},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:1","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { quote, claim } from \"./claim-office.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"MHPCO Claim Office\", () => {\n  describe(\"quote\", () => {\n    it(\"should return 5 G for empty item list -- edge case\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items: any[] = [];\n      expect(quote(customer, items)).toBe(5);\n    });\n    it(\"should return 105 G for a single plain sword -- base premium + fee\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\" }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items)).toBe(105);\n    });\n    it(\"should return 55 G for 2 runes -- 50 base premium + 5 fee\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items)).toBe(55);\n    });\n    it(\"should return 65 G for 3 runes -- 60 block premium + 5 fee\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items)).toBe(65);\n    });\n    it(\"should return 105 G for 4 runes -- 100 base + 5 fee, no block\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items)).toBe(105);\n    });\n    it(\"should return 180 G for 7 runes -- 175 base + 5 fee (two blocks + one extra)\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = Array(7).fill({ type: \"rune\" });\n      expect(quote(customer, items)).toBe(180);\n    });\n    it(\"should return 80 G for 2 runes + 1 moonstone -- 75 base + 5 fee, different types no block\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"moonstone\" }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items)).toBe(80);\n    });\n    it(\"should return 125 G for 3 runes + 3 moonstones -- 120 base + 5 fee (two blocks)\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n        { type: \"moonstone\" },\n        { type: \"moonstone\" },\n        { type: \"moonstone\" },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items)).toBe(125);\n    });\n    it(\"should apply cursed surcharge to affected item only -- cursed sword + plain amulet -> 231 G total with first insurance\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: true },\n        { type: \"amulet\", material: \"silver\", enchantment: 2, cursed: false },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(231);\n    });\n    it(\"should return 165 G for newcomer with a cursed sword -- integration example\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: true },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(165);\n    });\n    it(\"should return 160 G for long-standing customer's second contract cursed enchanted sword -- integration example\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 3 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 7, cursed: true },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 1)).toBe(160);\n    });\n    it(\"should apply high-enchantment surcharge for enchantment >= 5\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 5, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(145);\n    });\n    it(\"should not apply high-enchantment surcharge for enchantment 4\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 4, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(115);\n    });\n    it(\"should apply loyalty discount for customer with >= 2 years\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 2 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(95);\n    });\n    it(\"should apply first insurance surcharge for first contract\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(115);\n    });\n    it(\"should apply follow-up contract discount after first contract\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 1)).toBe(100);\n    });\n    it(\"should round premium up in MHPCO's favor -- 197.5 G -> 198 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = Array(7).fill({ type: \"rune\" });\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(198);\n    });\n    it(\"should throw for unknown item type in quote\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"broomstick\" }];\n      expect(() => quote(customer, items, 0)).toThrow();\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe(\"claim\", () => {\n    it(\"should pay out 400 G for regular steel sword enchantment 3 damage 500 G -- standard reimbursement\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        remainingCap: 2000,\n      };\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 });\n    });\n    it(\"should pay out 100 G for rune damage 200 G -- standard reimbursement\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [{ type: \"rune\" }],\n        remainingCap: 500,\n      };\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"rune\", amount: 200 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 100, remainingCap: 400 });\n    });\n    it(\"should pay out 400 G for dragon-material sword enchantment 9 damage 1000 G -- 50% rule wins over dragon\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 9, cursed: false }],\n        remainingCap: 2000,\n      };\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 });\n    });\n    it(\"should pay out 700 G for dragon-material sword enchantment 5 damage 800 G -- only dragon clause applies\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 5, cursed: false }],\n        remainingCap: 2000,\n      };\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 800 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 700, remainingCap: 1300 });\n    });\n    it(\"should pay out 400 G for steel sword enchantment 9 damage 1000 G -- only high-enchantment clause applies\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 9, cursed: false }],\n        remainingCap: 2000,\n      };\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 });\n    });\n    it(\"should apply 100 G deductible per damaged item -- dragon attack on sword and amulet -> 600 G\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [\n          { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n          { type: \"amulet\", material: \"silver\", enchantment: 2, cursed: false },\n        ],\n        remainingCap: 3200,\n      };\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"dragon attack\",\n        damages: [\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n          { itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 300 },\n        ],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 600, remainingCap: 2600 });\n    });\n    it(\"should treat multiple damages of same item type as separate events with separate deductibles\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [\n          { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n          { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n        ],\n        remainingCap: 4000,\n      };\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"dragon attack\",\n        damages: [\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n        ],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 800, remainingCap: 3200 });\n    });\n    it(\"should reject claim when damages exceed insured count of same type\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        remainingCap: 2000,\n      };\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"dragon attack\",\n        damages: [\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n        ],\n      };\n      expect(() => claim(policy, incident)).toThrow();\n    });\n    it(\"should cap total payout at twice the insurance sum -- two successive claims\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false }],\n        remainingCap: 2000,\n      };\n      const incident1 = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }],\n      };\n      const result1 = claim(policy, incident1);\n      expect(result1).toEqual({ payout: 1400, remainingCap: 600 });\n      const policy2 = { ...policy, remainingCap: result1.remainingCap };\n      const incident2 = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy2, incident2)).toEqual({ payout: 600, remainingCap: 0 });\n    });\n    it(\"should round payout down in MHPCO's favor -- 350.5 G -> 350 G\", () => {\n      const policy = {\n        items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 9, cursed: false }],\n        remainingCap: 2000,\n      };\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 901 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 350, remainingCap: 1650 });\n    });\n    it.todo(\"should reject claim for unknown item type in damages -- non-zero exit\");\n    it.todo(\"should reject claim for negative damage amount -- non-zero exit\");\n  });\n});\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784925008124},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the implementation and tests. Let me analyze the code for refactoring opportunities.\n\nFirst, let me run the tests to make sure they all pass currently.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.bash:2","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test","timeout":60}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":4511,"output":57,"cacheRead":5760,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":33,"totalTokens":10328,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784925008124,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-c61015b5-c948-40d3-a78c-a43193b07498","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.bash:2","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-41-05_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7\n> vitest run\n\n\n RUN  v1.6.1 /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-41-05_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7\n\n ✓ src/claim-office.spec.ts  (30 tests | 2 skipped) 5ms\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  28 passed | 2 todo (30)\n   Start at  20:30:09\n   Duration  171ms (transform 34ms, setup 0ms, collect 33ms, tests 5ms, environment 0ms, prepare 50ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784925009862},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, all 28 tests pass. Now let me analyze the code for refactoring opportunities following the Simple Design Rules.\n\n## Naming Evaluation\n\nLooking at the code, I need to evaluate if function names clearly reveal intent.\n\n1. `countWhere` - counts items matching predicate. Good name.\n2. `premiumForComponentCount` - calculates premium for a number of components. Good.\n3. `premiumForComponentsOfType` - calculates premium for components of a specific type. Good.\n4. `basePremiumForItem` - base premium for a single item. Good.\n5. `isKnownItemType` - checks if item type is known. Good.\n6. `validateKnownItemTypes` - validates known item types. Good.\n7. `componentPremiumTotal` - total component premium. Good.\n8. `basePremiumForAllItems` - base premium for all items. Good.\n9. `sumBy` - sums values extracted from items. Good.\n10. `hasEnchantmentAtLeast` - checks if enchantment >= threshold. Good.\n11. `surchargeRateFor` - surcharge rate for item. Good.\n12. `surchargeCentsFor` - surcharge in cents. Good.\n13. `roundUpCentsToGold` - rounds up cents to gold. Good.\n14. `policyAdjustmentRate` - calculates policy adjustment rate. Good.\n15. `quote` - calculates quote. Good.\n16. `findItemByType` - finds item by type. Good.\n17. `payoutMultiplierFor` - payout multiplier. Good.\n18. `damagePayout` - payout for damage. Good.\n19. `validateClaim` - validates claim. Good.\n20. `claim` - processes claim. Good.\n\nNames are generally good. \n\n## Potential Refactoring Opportunities\n\n1. **Duplication in `payoutMultiplierFor` and `surchargeRateFor`**: Both use `hasEnchantmentAtLeast` with different thresholds. Not much duplication though.\n\n2. **Duplication in validation**: `validateKnownItemTypes` and `validateClaim` both validate something. Not obvious duplication.\n\n3. **The `quote` function**: It mixes cents and gold conversions. Could be clearer.\n\n4. **`validateClaim`**: It has a set of damage types then iterates. Could be simpler. But it's clear.\n\n5. **`claim` function**: It creates `roundedPayout` then uses it twice. Could inline or keep. Actually, `roundedPayout` is an explaining variable that reveals intent.\n\n6. **Type definitions**: `Item` has optional `material` but `material` is never used in business logic. Wait, is it used? Let me check. No, `material` is in tests but not used in implementation. That's a smell but removing it would break test type data? Actually the tests use it but it's not required. But removing it isn't really a refactoring improvement.\n\n7. **`COMPONENT_TYPES` and `EQUIPMENT_BASE_PREMIUMS`**: Good constants.\n\n8. **Duplication in tests?** We're refactoring implementation, not tests.\n\nLet me think about conceptual duplication. In `claim`:\n```ts\nconst totalPayout = sumBy(incident.damages, (damage) =>\n  damagePayout(findItemByType(policy.items, damage.itemType), damage.amount),\n);\n```\n\nThis finds the item by type for each damage. But `validateClaim` already groups damages by type. Could we reuse that? Maybe not worth it.\n\nLooking at `damagePayout`:\n```ts\nconst damagePayout = (item: Item | undefined, amount: number): number =>\n  amount * payoutMultiplierFor(item) - DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD;\n```\n\nThis could potentially be negative (deductible > amount). Tests don't cover this but it's not our concern.\n\nA cleaner refactoring might be in `quote`:\n```ts\nconst totalCents =\n  baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + policyAdjustmentCents + BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\nreturn roundUpCentsToGold(totalCents);\n```\n\nWe could extract `BASE_FEE_CENTS` constant to avoid recomputation, but that's just one place.\n\nActually, a good refactoring opportunity: **extract `totalBaseCents` or `subtotalCents` calculation**. The name `totalCents` is slightly misleading because it includes fees and adjustments. Maybe `finalCents` or `grossTotalCents`.\n\nBut that's minor. Let me think of more substantive refactoring.\n\n**Idea: Combine `premiumForComponentCount` logic with more clarity**\n\nActually, looking at `policyAdjustmentRate`:\n```ts\nconst policyAdjustmentRate = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  contractIndex: number | null,\n): number => {\n  const qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_YEARS;\n  const hasInsuranceContract = contractIndex !== null;\n  const isFollowUpContract = hasInsuranceContract && contractIndex > 0;\n\n  return (\n    (qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount ? -LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0) +\n    (hasInsuranceContract ? INSURANCE_CONTRACT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0) +\n    (isFollowUpContract ? -FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0)\n  );\n};\n```\n\nThis is clear. But `hasInsuranceContract` could be `isFirstContract` or similar. Actually, `hasInsuranceContract` means `contractIndex !== null`. The surcharge applies when there's an insurance contract. The follow-up discount applies when contractIndex > 0. This is clear.\n\nWait, there's a subtle issue: the names `qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount`, `hasInsuranceContract`, `isFollowUpContract` are boolean explaining variables. They reveal intent well.\n\n**Idea for claim validation duplication reduction:**\n\nIn `validateClaim`, we count insured and damage counts per type. We use `countWhere` twice. Could extract a `countByType` helper:\n\n```ts\nconst countByType = (items: Item[], type: string): number =>\n  countWhere(items, (item) => item.type === type);\n```\n\nBut that's just a partial application wrapper. Maybe not worth it.\n\nActually, looking more carefully:\n\n```ts\nconst damageTypes = new Set(incident.damages.map((damage) => damage.itemType));\nfor (const type of damageTypes) {\n  const insuredCount = countWhere(policy.items, (item) => item.type === type);\n  const damageCount = countWhere(incident.damages, (damage) => damage.itemType === type);\n  if (damageCount > insuredCount) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage count exceeds insured count for ${type}`);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWe could extract a `countByProperty` or similar. But current code is fine.\n\n**Better idea: Extract a `payoutForDamage` function**\n\nIn `claim`:\n```ts\nconst totalPayout = sumBy(incident.damages, (damage) =>\n  damagePayout(findItemByType(policy.items, damage.itemType), damage.amount),\n);\n```\n\nThis could become:\n```ts\nconst totalPayout = sumBy(incident.damages, (damage) => payoutForDamage(policy.items, damage));\n```\n\nwith:\n```ts\nconst payoutForDamage = (items: Item[], damage: Damage): number =>\n  damagePayout(findItemByType(items, damage.itemType), damage.amount);\n```\n\nThis reduces the closure complexity in `claim` and makes the logic more testable/explicit. It reveals intent better: \"payout for this damage, given these insured items\".\n\n**Another idea: Rename `damagePayout` to `payoutForItemDamage` or `damagePayoutForItem`**\n\nThe function takes an item and an amount. `damagePayout` is okay but could be more specific.\n\n**Idea: The `roundUpCentsToGold` function**\n\nThis is used only in `quote`. Good name.\n\n**Idea: Simplify `sumBy` and `countWhere`**\n\nThese are generic utilities. Maybe they belong elsewhere? But for this kata, keeping them here is fine.\n\n**Idea: Remove `material` from `Item` type**\n\nThe `material` property is never used in business logic. However, removing it isn't really a refactoring that improves behavior clarity - it's removing unused type info. Tests pass objects with material, but TypeScript would allow extra properties. Wait, would it? In TypeScript, extra properties are allowed when assigned to a variable of a type (excess property checks only apply to object literals). So `{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\" }` as an object literal assigned to `Item` parameter would trigger excess property check if `material` isn't in `Item`.\n\nActually, let me check. In the tests, they pass objects directly to `quote(customer, items, 0)` where `items` is inferred as `{ type: string, material: string, enchantment: number, cursed: boolean }[]`. This doesn't go through an explicit `Item[]` annotation, so excess property checking might not apply. Wait, the function parameter is `insuredItems: Item[]`. When you call `quote(customer, [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", ... }])`, TypeScript checks the object literal against `Item`, and would complain about `material` if it's not in `Item`.\n\nBut the tests pass. So either `material` is in `Item` (it is), or TypeScript isn't strict. Since tests pass with `material` present, removing `material` from `Item` would likely cause TypeScript errors in tests.\n\nActually, the `Item` type currently has `material?: string`. If we remove it, the test object literals with `material` would fail excess property check. So we can't remove it without modifying tests, which we shouldn't do.\n\n**Idea: Better naming in `quote`**\n\nThe variable `totalCents` could be `finalCents` or `amountDueCents`. But `totalCents` is okay.\n\n**Idea: Extract `feeCents` constant**\n\nIn `quote`:\n```ts\nconst totalCents =\n  baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + policyAdjustmentCents + BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n```\n\nWe could add:\n```ts\nconst baseFeeCents = BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n```\n\nThis reveals intent: \"the base fee in cents\". It also reduces duplication if BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD were used elsewhere, but it's only here.\n\n**Idea: Combine the capping and flooring in claim**\n\n```ts\nconst cappedPayout = Math.min(totalPayout, policy.remainingCap);\nconst roundedPayout = Math.floor(cappedPayout);\nreturn { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - roundedPayout };\n```\n\nCould be:\n```ts\nconst payout = Math.floor(Math.min(totalPayout, policy.remainingCap));\nreturn { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n```\n\nThis reduces elements (Rule 4). But the explaining variables `cappedPayout` and `roundedPayout` reveal intent (Rule 2). Rule 2 trumps Rule 4, so keeping them might be better. However, since the recent green phase was \"Applied Math.floor to capped payout\", having both variables shows the two-step process clearly. I'd lean toward keeping them or slightly renaming.\n\nActually, a good refactoring: rename `roundedPayout` to `finalPayout` or `payout` since it's what's returned. But `roundedPayout` describes what was done. Hmm.\n\n**Idea: Extract helper for claim result creation**\n\n```ts\nreturn { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - roundedPayout };\n```\n\nThe remaining cap calculation is simple subtraction. No need to extract.\n\n**Idea: Use `reduce` instead of `sumBy`?**\n\nNo, `sumBy` is a good abstraction.\n\n**Idea: Type narrowing in `payoutMultiplierFor` and `surchargeRateFor`**\n\nThese both accept `Item | undefined`. The undefined handling is consistent. Good.\n\n**Idea: Rename `surchargeCentsFor` to `itemSurchargeCents`?**\n\nNo, `surchargeCentsFor` is good.\n\n**Idea: Consolidate constants related to same feature**\n\nNot necessary.\n\n**Strongest candidate: Extract `payoutForDamage`**\n\nThis reduces nesting in `claim`, makes the logic more readable, and reveals intent. It also slightly reduces mass by avoiding the inline closure.\n\nLet me also consider renaming `damagePayout` to `payoutForDamagedItem` or similar. But `damagePayout` is okay.\n\nActually, another idea: In `quote`, we calculate:\n```ts\nconst baseCents = basePremiumForAllItems(insuredItems) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\nconst itemSurchargeTotal = sumBy(insuredItems, surchargeCentsFor);\nconst policyRate = policyAdjustmentRate(customer, contractIndex);\nconst policyAdjustmentCents = baseCents * policyRate;\nconst totalCents =\n  baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + policyAdjustmentCents + BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\nreturn roundUpCentsToGold(totalCents);\n```\n\nThe pattern \"calculate cents, then convert\" is consistent. We could extract a helper `inCents(gold)` or `toCents(goldAmount)`. But that might add an element without much benefit.\n\nWait, there IS a duplication: `* CENTS_PER_GOLD` appears 3 times (in `baseCents`, `surchargeCentsFor`, and `totalCents`). The one in `surchargeCentsFor` is internal, so we can't easily remove it without changing the function. The other two are in `quote`. We could extract:\n\n```ts\nconst toCents = (gold: number): number => gold * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n```\n\nThen:\n```ts\nconst baseCents = toCents(basePremiumForAllItems(insuredItems));\nconst baseFeeCents = toCents(BASE_FEE);\nconst totalCents = baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + policyAdjustmentCents + baseFeeCents;\n```\n\nThis reduces duplication of `* CENTS_PER_GOLD` and reveals intent (convert gold to cents). However, it adds a function. Mass impact: removing 2 invocations of `*` (each is a binding? no, `*` is an operator, not counted in APP mass per the given components). Wait, let me reconsider APP.\n\nAPP components:\n- Constant: literal values\n- Binding/Scalar: variables, parameters\n- Invocation: function calls\n- Conditional: if, switch, ?:\n- Loop: for, forEach, map, reduce\n- Assignment: mutations\n\n`*` is an operator, not counted. `CENTS_PER_GOLD` is a binding (1). So replacing `gold * CENTS_PER_GOLD` with `toCents(gold)` would be: remove binding `CENTS_PER_GOLD` reference? No, it's still there in `toCents`. Actually, mass of `basePremiumForAllItems(insuredItems) * CENTS_PER_GOLD`:\n- Constants: 0\n- Bindings: 2 (`CENTS_PER_GOLD` is a binding reference, the expression has a parameter but it's in the function call)\nWait, how do we count? Let me think about APP mass for an expression.\n\nFor `items.filter(predicate).length`:\n- Constants: 0\n- Bindings: 3 (items, predicate, length property? Hmm)\n- Invocations: 2 (filter, length property access? property access is not invocation)\nActually, this is tricky. Let me not get too bogged down in exact counts but use APP as a rough guide.\n\n**Key refactoring candidates:**\n1. Extract `toCents(gold)` helper - reduces conceptual duplication of conversion logic\n2. Extract `payoutForDamage(items, damage)` - improves readability of `claim`\n3. Rename `damagePayout` to something clearer\n\nI think the best refactoring is a combination:\n- Extract `toCents` helper (DRY gold-to-cents conversion in quote)\n- Extract `payoutForDamage` helper (improve claim readability)\n\nBut the instruction says \"Make ONE improvement at a time\". I'll choose the most valuable one.\n\nActually, looking again, the `toCents` extraction is nice because:\n1. It removes duplication of the conversion logic\n2. It reveals intent: \"convert gold amount to cents\"\n3. It's used in `quote` for base premium and base fee\n\nBut wait, `surchargeCentsFor` also does `* CENTS_PER_GOLD`. If we use `toCents` there too, we'd have:\n```ts\nconst surchargeCentsFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  toCents(basePremiumForItem(item) * surchargeRateFor(item));\n```\n\nThat would consolidate all `* CENTS_PER_GOLD` usage. That's a strong DRY improvement.\n\nHowever, changing `surchargeCentsFor` to use `toCents` might slightly obscure the math. But the name `toCents` reveals intent, so it might improve clarity.\n\n**Alternative: Extract `goldToCents` or `asCents`**\n\nName should be clear. `toCents` is concise. `asCents` suggests a view, not conversion. `goldToCents` is explicit. I'll use `toCents`.\n\nLet me plan the refactoring:\n\n1. Add `toCents` helper:\n```ts\nconst toCents = (gold: number): number => gold * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n```\n\n2. Update `surchargeCentsFor`:\n```ts\nconst surchargeCentsFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  toCents(basePremiumForItem(item) * surchargeRateFor(item));\n```\n\n3. Update `quote`:\n```ts\nconst baseCents = toCents(basePremiumForAllItems(insuredItems));\nconst itemSurchargeTotal = sumBy(insuredItems, surchargeCentsFor);\nconst policyRate = policyAdjustmentRate(customer, contractIndex);\nconst policyAdjustmentCents = baseCents * policyRate;\nconst baseFeeCents = toCents(BASE_FEE);\nconst totalCents = baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + policyAdjustmentCents + baseFeeCents;\nreturn roundUpCentsToGold(totalCents);\n```\n\nThis removes 3 references to `CENTS_PER_GOLD` multiplication in favor of `toCents`. It adds 1 function and 2 invocations.\n\nWait, actually this changes mass slightly. Let me roughly estimate.\n\nBefore:\n- `surchargeCentsFor`: 1 binding (CENTS_PER_GOLD) + 2 invocations (basePremiumForItem, surchargeRateFor) + 1 multiplication operator (not counted)\n- `quote`: 2 bindings (CENTS_PER_GOLD) + invocations\n\nAfter:\n- `toCents`: 1 binding (CENTS_PER_GOLD), 1 parameter (gold)\n- `surchargeCentsFor`: 1 invocation (toCents) + 2 invocations (basePremiumForItem, surchargeRateFor)\n- `quote`: 2 invocations (toCents)\n\nHmm, it's not clear if mass goes down. But APP is a guide, Rule 2 and Rule 3 matter more. The DRY improvement and intent revelation are valuable.\n\nLet me also consider extracting `payoutForDamage`. It would:\n- Make `claim` read: `const totalPayout = sumBy(incident.damages, (damage) => payoutForDamage(policy.items, damage));`\n- Add function: `const payoutForDamage = (items: Item[], damage: Damage): number => damagePayout(findItemByType(items, damage.itemType), damage.amount);`\n\nThis improves readability of the `sumBy` call. The closure in `sumBy` currently has nested function calls which is a bit dense. Extracting `payoutForDamage` reveals intent.\n\nI think I'll do both refactorings since they're in different functions and both small. But the instruction says \"Make ONE improvement at a time\". I'll do `toCents` first, run tests, then `payoutForDamage`, run tests. Actually, I can combine them in one edit since they're separate and small. But to be safe, I'll do them sequentially.\n\nActually, looking at the APP mass calculation requirement, I should calculate before and after. If I do two changes, I'll show combined effect. That's fine.\n\nLet me calculate initial APP mass first.\n\n## Initial APP Mass Calculation\n\nThis is tricky because APP counting isn't precisely defined for TypeScript syntax. Let me count conservatively for the implementation file.\n\nActually, I'll focus on the functions being changed to keep it manageable. But the instructions say calculate code mass. Let me do a rough count for the whole file or at least the relevant functions.\n\nFor `surchargeCentsFor`:\n```ts\nconst surchargeCentsFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  basePremiumForItem(item) * surchargeRateFor(item) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n```\n- Constants: 0\n- Bindings: 2 (item, CENTS_PER_GOLD)\n- Invocations: 2 (basePremiumForItem, surchargeRateFor)\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 0\nTotal: 6\n\nFor `quote`:\n```ts\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  insuredItems: Item[],\n  contractIndex: number | null = null,\n): number => {\n  validateKnownItemTypes(insuredItems);\n  const baseCents = basePremiumForAllItems(insuredItems) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n  const itemSurchargeTotal = sumBy(insuredItems, surchargeCentsFor);\n  const policyRate = policyAdjustmentRate(customer, contractIndex);\n  const policyAdjustmentCents = baseCents * policyRate;\n  const totalCents =\n    baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + policyAdjustmentCents + BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n  return roundUpCentsToGold(totalCents);\n};\n```\n- Constants: 1 (null default)\n- Bindings: 6 (customer, insuredItems, contractIndex, baseCents, itemSurchargeTotal, policyRate, policyAdjustmentCents, totalCents) = 8\n- Invocations: 6 (validateKnownItemTypes, basePremiumForAllItems, sumBy, policyAdjustmentRate, roundUpCentsToGold)\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 4 (const declarations are bindings, but the `=` is assignment? In APP, \"assignments\" refers to mutations like `x = 5` or `count++`. `const` declarations might be counted as bindings. I'll count const declarations as bindings, not assignments.)\nTotal: 1 + 8 + 12 = 21\n\nFor `claim`:\n```ts\nexport const claim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult => {\n  validateClaim(policy, incident);\n  const totalPayout = sumBy(incident.damages, (damage) =>\n    damagePayout(findItemByType(policy.items, damage.itemType), damage.amount),\n  );\n  const cappedPayout = Math.min(totalPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  const roundedPayout = Math.floor(cappedPayout);\n  return { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - roundedPayout };\n};\n```\n- Constants: 0\n- Bindings: 5 (policy, incident, totalPayout, cappedPayout, roundedPayout, damage parameter) = 6\n- Invocations: 5 (validateClaim, sumBy, damagePayout, findItemByType, Math.min, Math.floor) = 6\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Loops: 0 (sumBy has reduce internally but we're counting the file's surface)\n- Assignments: 0\nTotal: 6 + 12 = 18\n\nHmm, this APP counting is ambiguous. The instructions show counting reduce as a loop. But for our functions, the loops are