suredItems))` vs `basePremiumForAllItems(insuredItems) * CENTS_PER_GOLD`. The latter is arguably just as clear, maybe more so because it shows the conversion explicitly.\n\nActually, I think extracting `toCents` might not improve clarity much. The constant `CENTS_PER_GOLD` already reveals intent. Multiplication by it is a standard conversion.\n\nWhat about extracting `payoutForDamage`?\n\nBefore (claim):\n- bindings 6, invocations 6 → 18\n\nAfter (claim + payoutForDamage):\n- payoutForDamage: bindings 3 (items, damage, plus destructured? No), invocations 2 (damagePayout, findItemByType) → wait, plus references to damage.itemType and damage.amount? Those are property accesses, not bindings? Hmm.\n  If we write:\n  ```ts\n  const payoutForDamage = (items: Item[], damage: Damage): number =>\n    damagePayout(findItemByType(items, damage.itemType), damage.amount);\n  ```\n  Bindings: items, damage (2). Invocations: damagePayout, findItemByType (2). Property accesses don't count as bindings? The instructions don't say. I'll not count property accesses. Total: 2 + 4 = 6.\n- claim: bindings 6, invocations 5 (validateClaim, sumBy, payoutForDamage, Math.min, Math.floor) → 6 + 10 = 16\nTotal: 6 + 16 = 22\n\nSo mass goes from 18 to 22, an increase. But readability improves.\n\nHmm, both refactorings increase mass. But Rule 2 says clarity over mass.\n\nActually, maybe I should look for a refactoring that DECREASES mass or at least doesn't increase it much, while improving clarity.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe I should focus on naming improvements which don't change mass much.\n\n**Idea: Rename `damagePayout` to `calculateDamagePayout` or `payoutForDamagedItem`**\n\nBut the function calculates the payout for damage to an item. `damagePayout` is okay. Maybe `damagePayoutFor`? Not much better.\n\n**Idea: Rename `totalCents` to `grossTotalCents` or `amountDueCents`**\n\nThis is a naming improvement that reveals intent: this is the final amount due in cents. But `totalCents` already conveys that.\n\n**Idea: Rename `roundedPayout` to `finalPayout` or just `payout`**\n\nThe current name says it was rounded, which is implementation detail. The returned object has `payout`, so `finalPayout` might be better. But `roundedPayout` explains the Math.floor step.\n\n**Idea: Inline the simple helper functions?**\n\nNo, that would increase duplication.\n\n**Idea: Simplify `validateClaim` using `countByType` helper**\n\n```ts\nconst countByType = (items: Item[], type: string): number =>\n  countWhere(items, (item) => item.type === type);\n```\n\nThen:\n```ts\nconst validateClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): void => {\n  const damageTypes = new Set(incident.damages.map((damage) => damage.itemType));\n  for (const type of damageTypes) {\n    const insuredCount = countByType(policy.items, type);\n    const damageCount = countByType(incident.damages, type);\n    if (damageCount > insuredCount) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage count exceeds insured count for ${type}`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n```\n\nThis reduces conceptual duplication (same lambda used twice) and reveals intent: \"count items of this type\". But it adds a helper.\n\nMass:\nBefore (validateClaim):\n- bindings: policy, incident, damageTypes, type, insuredCount, damageCount → 6\n- invocations: new Set, map, countWhere (×2) → 4\n- loops: 1 (for...of)\n- conditionals: 1 (if)\nTotal: 6 + 8 + 5 + 4 = 23\n\nAfter (validateClaim + countByType):\n- countByType: bindings 3 (items, type, predicate? no, just items, type), invocations 1 (countWhere) → 3 + 2 = 5\n- validateClaim: bindings 6, invocations 4 (Set, map, countByType ×2), loops 1, conditionals 1 → 6 + 8 + 5 + 4 = 23\nTotal: 28\n\nMass increases but duplication is reduced. Hmm.\n\n**Idea: A mass-reducing refactoring**\n\nCan I find one? \n\nLooking at `policyAdjustmentRate`:\n```ts\nconst policyAdjustmentRate = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  contractIndex: number | null,\n): number => {\n  const qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_YEARS;\n  const hasInsuranceContract = contractIndex !== null;\n  const isFollowUpContract = hasInsuranceContract && contractIndex > 0;\n\n  return (\n    (qualifiesForLoyaltyDiscount ? -LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0) +\n    (hasInsuranceContract ? INSURANCE_CONTRACT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0) +\n    (isFollowUpContract ? -FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0)\n  );\n};\n```\n\nWe could inline the booleans to reduce mass:\n```ts\nconst policyAdjustmentRate = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  contractIndex: number | null,\n): number =>\n  (customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_YEARS ? -LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0) +\n  (contractIndex !== null ? INSURANCE_CONTRACT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0) +\n  (contractIndex !== null && contractIndex > 0 ? -FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0);\n```\n\nThis removes 3 bindings (the const variables) and keeps the same logic. Mass decrease:\nBefore: constants 0, bindings 5 (customer, contractIndex, qualifies..., has..., is...), invocations 0, conditionals 3, loops 0 → 5 + 12 = 17\nAfter: bindings 2 (customer, contractIndex), conditionals 3 → 2 + 12 = 14\nDecrease by 3.\n\nBut does this hurt clarity? The original has explaining variables that reveal intent. Rule 2 says clarity trumps APP. So inlining would reduce clarity (the booleans explain what each condition means). So this is NOT a good refactoring.\n\n**Idea: Simplify `quote` by inlining `policyAdjustmentCents`**\n\n```ts\nconst policyAdjustmentCents = baseCents * policyRate;\n```\n\nCould inline:\n```ts\nconst totalCents =\n  baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + baseCents * policyRate + baseFeeCents;\n```\n\nThis removes one binding and one assignment. Mass decrease. But it reduces clarity because `policyAdjustmentCents` explains the meaning. Not worth it.\n\n**Idea: A good mass-reducing refactoring that preserves clarity**\n\nLet me look at `premiumForComponentCount`:\n```ts\nconst premiumForComponentCount = (count: number): number => {\n  if (count === COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM;\n  }\n  return count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n};\n```\n\nThis is clear. Could use ternary:\n```ts\nconst premiumForComponentCount = (count: number): number =>\n  count === COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n```\n\nMass:\nBefore: bindings 1 (count), conditionals 1 (if) → 1 + 4 = 5\nAfter: bindings 1 (count), conditionals 1 (ternary) → 1 + 4 = 5\nSame mass. Ternary is more concise but maybe less clear. Not a strong win.\n\n**Idea: Combine `basePremiumForAllItems` and `componentPremiumTotal`**\n\n```ts\nconst basePremiumForAllItems = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  sumBy(items, basePremiumForItem) + componentPremiumTotal(items);\n```\n\nThis is already simple. \n\n**Idea: Extract `isComponent` predicate**\n\n```ts\nconst isComponent = (item: Item): boolean => COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type);\n```\n\nUsed where? In `isKnownItemType` and `premiumForComponentsOfType` (indirectly). Not much.\n\n**Idea: Use destructuring in `claim` result**\n\n```ts\nreturn { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - roundedPayout };\n```\n\nCould be:\n```ts\nconst remainingCap = policy.remainingCap - roundedPayout;\nreturn { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap };\n```\n\nThis adds a binding but reveals intent. Mass increase. Not worth it.\n\n**Idea: Better mass-reducing change**\n\nWhat if I look at the whole file and remove unused constants or functions? \n\n- `CENTS_PER_GOLD` is used multiple times. Keep.\n- All constants seem used.\n\n- `isKnownItemType` and `validateKnownItemTypes` are used. Keep.\n\n- `material` property is unused in logic but present in type. Can't remove without test changes.\n\nHmm.\n\n**Idea: Combine the Set creation and loop in `validateClaim` differently?**\n\nNo.\n\n**Idea: Use `filter` + `length` instead of `countWhere`?**\n\nThat would be worse.\n\n**Idea: Rename functions for clarity (no mass change)**\n\nThis is a good, safe refactoring. Names are important per Rule 2.\n\nCandidates:\n- `damagePayout` → `payoutForDamagedItem` (more specific)\n- `totalCents` variable in `quote` → `grossCents` or `amountDueCents` (more specific)\n- `roundedPayout` variable in `claim` → `finalPayout` (less implementation-detail)\n\nBut are these improvements? Let me think.\n\n`damagePayout(item, amount)` - the function takes an item and amount. It returns the payout for that damage. `payoutForDamagedItem` might be better. But `damagePayout` is also fine.\n\n`totalCents` - this is the total amount in cents before rounding. The name is okay.\n\n`roundedPayout` - this is the payout after applying cap and floor. `finalPayout` or `payout` would be more focused on the result, but `roundedPayout` explains the Math.floor. Given the test \"should round payout down in MHPCO's favor\", the rounding is important. `roundedPayout` is good.\n\nHmm, naming improvements are marginal.\n\n**Idea: Move `sumBy` to use `reduce`?**\n\nNo, `sumBy` is good.\n\n**Idea: A real improvement - remove the `Set` from `validateClaim`?**\n\nActually, we can simplify `validateClaim` to not build a Set:\n\n```ts\nconst validateClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): void => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of policy.items) {\n    counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const remaining = counts.get(damage.itemType) ?? 0;\n    if (remaining <= 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage count exceeds insured count for ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    counts.set(damage.itemType, remaining - 1);\n  }\n};\n```\n\nThis is more efficient and arguably clearer (one pass over damages). But it uses a Map and mutations. Mass:\n- bindings: policy, incident, counts, item, damage, remaining → 6\n- invocations: new Map, set (×2), get (×2) → 5\n- loops: 2 (for...of ×2)\n- conditionals: 1 (if)\n- assignments: 1 (counts.set)\nTotal: 6 + 10 + 10 + 4 + 6 = 36. Higher mass.\n\nBut it removes the Set and countWhere usage. The current implementation is clearer with countWhere. Not worth it.\n\n**Idea: Focus on DRY with `toCents` but accept mass increase for clarity**\n\nI think extracting `toCents` is a legitimate refactoring because:\n1. It centralizes the conversion logic (DRY)\n2. The name `toCents` reveals intent\n3. It makes future changes easier (if conversion logic changes)\n\nEven though mass might slightly increase, Rule 2 and Rule 3 justify it.\n\nBut wait, is it really DRY? `* CENTS_PER_GOLD` is a simple multiplication. DRY applies more to \"knowledge\" than to syntax. The knowledge \"convert gold to cents by multiplying by 100\" appears in multiple places. Centralizing it is reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, I could extract `baseFeeCents` constant without extracting `toCents`:\n```ts\nconst baseFeeCents = BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n```\n\nThis adds one binding but removes one multiplication instance from `quote` and makes the base fee in cents explicit. Mass:\nBefore quote: bindings 8, invocations 6, constants 1 → 21\nAfter quote: bindings 9, invocations 6, constants 1 → 22\nMass +1.\n\nNot a big deal. But it reveals intent: \"base fee converted to cents\". Combined with `toCents`, it would be even cleaner.\n\n**Idea: Extract `toCents` AND `payoutForDamage`**\n\nI'll do both. The combined mass increase is acceptable for the clarity/DRY gains.\n\nBut wait, I want to make sure I don't break tests. Let me implement carefully.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the `toCents` extraction. In `surchargeCentsFor`, the function is:\n```ts\nconst surchargeCentsFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  basePremiumForItem(item) * surchargeRateFor(item) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n```\n\nIf I change it to:\n```ts\nconst surchargeCentsFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  toCents(basePremiumForItem(item) * surchargeRateFor(item));\n```\n\nThis says \"convert the surcharge (in gold) to cents\". But the surcharge is `basePremiumForItem(item) * surchargeRateFor(item)`, which is a rate applied to base premium, resulting in gold. Converting to cents makes sense.\n\nThis is clear. I'll proceed.\n\nFor `payoutForDamage`:\n```ts\nconst payoutForDamage = (items: Item[], damage: Damage): number =>\n  damagePayout(findItemByType(items, damage.itemType), damage.amount);\n```\n\nThis reveals intent in `claim`:\n```ts\nconst totalPayout = sumBy(incident.damages, (damage) =>\n  payoutForDamage(policy.items, damage),\n);\n```\n\nThis is much clearer than the nested closure.\n\nNow, should I also rename `damagePayout`? Maybe not. `damagePayout` takes an item and amount, returns payout. `payoutForDamage` takes items and damage, finds item, calls `damagePayout`. The names distinguish the two levels. Good.\n\nWait, I could rename `damagePayout` to `payoutForItemDamage` to be more specific. But it's okay.\n\nLet me also consider extracting `payoutForDamage` vs inlining `damagePayout`. If I inline `damagePayout` into `payoutForDamage`:\n```ts\nconst payoutForDamage = (items: Item[], damage: Damage): number =>\n  damage.amount * payoutMultiplierFor(findItemByType(items, damage.itemType)) - DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD;\n```\n\nThen remove `damagePayout`. This would reduce one function. But it makes `payoutForDamage` more complex. Not worth it.\n\nActually, maybe `damagePayout` and `payoutForDamage` are too similar and confusing. Hmm. `damagePayout` is the low-level calculation. `payoutForDamage` is the higher-level lookup + calculation. Maybe rename `damagePayout` to `calculateItemDamagePayout`?\n\nI'll keep `damagePayout` and add `payoutForDamage`. The naming is okay.\n\nActually, let me think of a simpler, more impactful refactoring.\n\n**Idea: Extract `remainingCapAfterPayout` calculation**\n\n```ts\nreturn { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - roundedPayout };\n```\n\nThis is simple. No need.\n\n**Idea: Extract `capPayout` and `roundPayoutDown` helpers?**\n\nNo, that would add unnecessary abstraction.\n\n**Idea: Extract `deductible` as a function of nothing?**\n\nNo.\n\nLet me just proceed with:\n1. Extract `toCents` helper\n2. Use `toCents` in `surchargeCentsFor` and `quote`\n3. Extract `baseFeeCents` in `quote`\n4. Extract `payoutForDamage` helper\n5. Use `payoutForDamage` in `claim`\n\nI'll run tests after.\n\nWait, I need to be careful. The instructions say \"Make ONE improvement at a time\". If I combine multiple small refactorings into one edit, is that okay? They're separate concerns but both small. To be safe, I'll do the `toCents` extraction first, run tests, then do `payoutForDamage`, run tests.\n\nActually, since the task is autonomous refactoring, I can make all changes and run tests once. But to follow the spirit of \"one improvement at a time\", I'll do them sequentially.\n\nLet me start with `toCents`.\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\nCurrent names:\n- `quote` - calculates insurance quote\n- `claim` - processes insurance claim\n- `surchargeCentsFor` - converts item surcharge to cents\n- `damagePayout` - calculates payout for damage to an item\n\nAssessment:\n- All names are reasonably clear\n- `quote` and `claim` are concise domain terms\n- `surchargeCentsFor` clearly indicates it returns cents\n- `damagePayout` is okay but could be more specific\n\nDecision: Keep current names; add `toCents` helper to reveal conversion intent.\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\nI'll calculate mass for the functions we'll modify: `surchargeCentsFor` and `quote`.\n\n**Current Code Mass for surchargeCentsFor + quote:**\n\n```ts\nconst surchargeCentsFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  basePremiumForItem(item) * surchargeRateFor(item) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  insuredItems: Item[],\n  contractIndex: number | null = null,\n): number => {\n  validateKnownItemTypes(insuredItems);\n  const baseCents = basePremiumForAllItems(insuredItems) * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n  const itemSurchargeTotal = sumBy(insuredItems, surchargeCentsFor);\n  const policyRate = policyAdjustmentRate(customer, contractIndex);\n  const policyAdjustmentCents = baseCents * policyRate;\n  const totalCents =\n    baseCents + itemSurchargeTotal + policyAdjustmentCents + BASE_FEE * CENTS_PER_GOLD;\n  return roundUpCentsToGold(totalCents);\n};\n```\n\nComponent Count:\n- Constants: 1 (null default) = 1\n- Bindings: 10 (item, customer, insuredItems, contractIndex, baseCents, itemSurchargeTotal, policyRate, policyAdjustmentCents, totalCents, CENTS_PER_GOLD refs) = 10\n- Invocations: 8 (basePremiumForItem, surchargeRateFor, validateKnownItemTypes, basePremiumForAllItems, sumBy, policyAdjustmentRate, roundUpCentsToGold, plus CENTS_PER_GOLD? No, CENTS_PER_GOLD is binding) = 8\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 0 = 0\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\nTotal Mass: 27\n\nActually, I'm not sure how to count property/variable references consistently. Let me just count:\n- `surchargeCentsFor`: item (1), basePremiumForItem (1), surchargeRateFor (1), CENTS_PER_GOLD (1) = 4 bindings? No, function invocations are separate.\nLet me simplify:\n- `surchargeCentsFor`: bindings=1 (item), invocations=2 (basePremiumForItem, surchargeRateFor) → mass = 1 + 4 = 5\n- `quote`: bindings=8 (customer, insuredItems, contractIndex, baseCents, itemSurchargeTotal, policyRate, policyAdjustmentCents, totalCents), invocations=6, constants=1 (null) → mass = 8 + 12 + 1 = 21\nTotal: 26\n\nI'll use 26 as the baseline for the affected region.\n\nFor `claim` + `damagePayout`:\n- `damagePayout`: bindings=2 (item, amount), invocations=1 (payoutMultiplierFor) → 2 + 2 = 4\n- `claim`: bindings=6 (policy, incident, totalPayout, cappedPayout, roundedPayout, damage), invocations=6 (validateClaim, sumBy, damagePayout, findItemByType, Math.min, Math.floor) → 6 + 12 = 18\nTotal: 22\n\nBaseline total for affected regions: 48.\n\nNow let me implement.\n\nActually, I'll just edit the file and then run tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:3","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office